      "content": "The corporate opposition often framed their stance not as legitimate business practice, but as a defense of a flawed economic system that favored capital over human dignity. By resisting fair labor standards, corporations effectively argued for an economic model where individual workers were mere disposable resources, not citizens. This ideology undermined democracy by prioritizing unchecked private profit above public welfare and fundamental human rights. In essence, their resistance was a defense of plutocracy over participatory governance.",
      "source": "target",
